issue_comments.body:
Merged [PR #329](https://github.com/LightChainr/Matching-One/pull/329) via squash as [`7da6013`](https://github.com/LightChainr/Matching-One/commit/7da6013c347d767130d32a42bff2d7b6d36fd608) after the complete CI matrix passed on the rebased head (Python 3.9/3.11/3.13 and C++17).

Exact conclusion delivered by this slice:
- restricted-growth strings now give a unique encoding for every nonempty terminal set partition;
- enumeration returns exactly Bell(3)=5 and Bell(4)=15 encodings;
- terminal permutations use the frozen old-terminal-to-new-terminal convention, and supplied groups fail closed unless they contain a bijective identity and are closed under inverse and composition;
- orbit canonicalization is the lexicographically least RGS under the explicitly supplied group; no full-symmetric-group assumption is implicit;
- under S3 and S4, the audited orbit counts are respectively 3 and 5, with checked-in canonical representatives.

Unresolved boundary: this primitive does not enumerate gadget graphs, test planarity or graph isomorphism, evaluate probability polynomials, checkpoint a search, or establish a replacement-gadget result. Those remaining deliverables keep this parent issue open.

Claiming a bounded exact candidate-space census on branch `tools/issue-13-bounded-gadget-census`.

Scope:
- exhaust every labeled simple graph with three or four terminals and exactly one internal vertex;
- quotient by the full terminal symmetric group using the canonical graph encoding already on main;
- freeze orbit multiplicities and edge-count histograms;
- apply only explicit structural filters: connected carrier, and a stricter degree-`>=3` internal-vertex filter that removes leaf/series-like one-internal-vertex cases;
- emit a machine-readable exact census and deterministic regression tests, including reconstruction of the complete labeled space.

Out of scope: probability polynomials, planarity/tiling certification, general series-parallel reduction, self-duality, critical-manifold equations, ranking by threshold proximity, optimization, or any new percolation bound. Latest main, all open PRs, and branch searches for gadget/enumeration/series work show no overlapping implementation. The parent issue will remain open.

Complement-involution subtask completed in PR #391, squash-merged as `d174f21f263b12865864eb145193658e67af0df2` after full CI passed (C++17 and Python 3.9/3.11/3.13; run 33308837366).

Exact conclusions:
- complementation commutes with every audited terminal relabeling and descends to an involution on canonical graph orbits;
- for three terminals, all 20 orbits form 10 complement pairs and there are no self-complementary orbits;
- for four terminals, 90 orbits split into 44 complement pairs plus 2 self-complementary orbits;
- edge count is reversed `k -> M-k`, giving exact palindromic orbit histograms;
- exhaustive commutation tests cover all `64*6 + 1024*24 = 25,344` labeled graph/permutation combinations.

Validation: focused plus adjacent tests 21/21, artifact validation and compilation passed, complete CI green.

Boundary: complement is not claimed to preserve the connected filter and is not identified with planar/probability duality. No tiling, critical-manifold, ranking, threshold, or bound result. Keeping #13 open.
